# Cyclone (Khan, Anand, Brown and Baker, arXiv:2511.15910), as the team's transport deck writes it
# for QCCD Studio: one closed ring of traps, the data standing still and the ancillas rotating in
# lockstep.  Not the rule set `cyclone`, which is the paper's artifact as Reproduce replays it.
#
# Y1-Y4 are the deck's S1-S3 and HW1 where no R rule says the same thing.  The deck's S4 is R4b,
# its S5 R27 and R12, its HW3 R3 and R5.  Its S6 assigns stabilizers on the fly under R26: Goal 3
# keeps the circuit's order instead (the project owner's decision, 2026-11-06), so this set uses
# R25.  Its S8 and S9 are design choices, and its S10 assumes cooling, so nothing here heats and
# R7 holds at no cost.  R13 is left out: HW1 allows 30 ions a trap, which is Y4.

ruleset "cyclone-deck"
title "Cyclone's rules, as the deck writes them"
about "One closed ring of traps with L-shaped junctions at its corners. Data qubits never move. Every ancilla moves in lockstep, one trap per step, one way round. A trap does one operation at a time, and a step's gates come before its rotation. Each qubit's operations keep the circuit's order."

use R0, R1, R2, R3, R4, R4b, R5, R6, R6b, R7, R8, R11, R12, R18, R19, R20, R21, R24, R25, R27

param serial_kinds = {"gate2", "split", "merge"}
use Y1, Y2, Y3, Y4
